  "worked_solution": "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $10,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$, $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 5,$ and $\\pm 10.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $1,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{10}{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to their lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm 2, & \\pm 5, & \\pm 10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. \n\nThis makes a total of $8$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 4=8.$)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
